--- a/patches/spec-decode-int8-kv.patch
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,227 +0,0 @@
-Let the split-KV verify attention read an int8 per-token-head KV cache, and hook it into the
-Triton backend. This is what makes a quantized KV cache -- and therefore >64k context -- worth
-anything for SPEC=dflash2; hybrid-sw-block-promote.patch is what makes it fit.
-
-Two halves:
-
-1. v1/attention/ops/spec_decode_attn.py gains a QUANT path. TRITON_ATTN's
-   `int8_per_token_head` cache hands out K/V views of shape [num_blocks, block_size, Hkv,
-   D + 4] -- the head dim padded so one fp32 scale per (token, head) sits inline after the
-   data -- plus f32 scale views [num_blocks, block_size, Hkv]. The padded head dim is already
-   carried by stride_kh, so the addressing is untouched; only the element type and the scale
-   multiply change. Both scales are constant along D, so folding them in after each dot is
-   exact rather than an approximation:
-       s   = (q . k_int8) * k_scale      instead of   q . (k_int8 * k_scale)
-       acc = (p * v_scale) . v_int8      instead of   p . (v_int8 * v_scale)
-   int8 -> bf16 is itself exact (bf16 has 8 mantissa bits, int8 needs 7), so the only new
-   rounding is the one bf16 multiply vLLM's own Triton kernel also does. Measured against a
-   dequantized reference: max error 1e-4. Against the unquantized reference: 1.02% mean
-   relative, which is int8 quantization of the cache, not the kernel.
-
-2. v1/attention/backends/triton_attn.py calls it for multi-query batches, the way
-   flash_attn.py already does. vLLM's own unified attention cannot cover this case: it
-   disables its split-KV ("3D") path whenever max_seqlen_q > 1, and every DFlash2 step is a
-   multi-query verify. Measured per attention layer at a 128k context, 8 query tokens:
-   1.3 ms for this kernel against 7.4 ms for unified attention and 10.1 ms for FA2. The gate
-   excludes the drafter's own sliding-window layers (they set a window) and everything the
-   kernel does not implement.
-
-Cost, honestly: the padded head dim means stride_kh is 260 B, so odd KV heads start 4 bytes
-off a 16-byte boundary and the vectorized loads break. In isolation that costs 61% (128k,
-q=8: 2,151 us against a contiguous int8 cache's 1,335 and bf16's 1,250); end to end it is
-about 13% of the step. Note also that a contiguous int8 cache is still slightly *slower* than
-bf16 despite half the bytes -- this kernel is tile-bound, not bandwidth-bound, at these
-shapes, so a quantized cache buys context, never speed. Reading the data as int32 (4-byte
-aligned, which 260 does satisfy) and unpacking would recover most of the 61%; not done here.
-
-Apply from the repo root, after spec-decode-attn.patch:
-  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/spec-decode-int8-kv.patch
-
-Written against vLLM 0.27.1. Reapply after upgrades.
